\clearpage
\subsection{Dependent Variables}


\subsubsection{Candidate Emergence}

To understand candidacy it is important to consider measures of candidate emergence 
that make the most sense. % Find studies of candidate emergence in low-level elections
Previous research has focused on incumbent challenges or candidate self-reports of 
contestation in their most recent elections \citep{RefWorks:311, RefWorks:321}. 
By collecting election results directly, in contrast to surveying former school 
board members, we can for the first time explore the level of contestation 
in school board elections directly. This allows me to construct measures of contestation, 
the presence of multiple choices for voters on the ballot. Choice among candidates 
is a key feature of a democratic body, and one criticism leveled against 
school boards and other local offices is that they are undemocratic due to a lack 
of choice for voters. %TODO: Citation here
Thus, a measure of contestation should capture the cases in which voters have a 
choice -- whether for an open seat, or a race contesting a seated incumbent. To 
that end, I use election results to construct two measures of contestation -- 
if the number of candidates is greater than the number of victors, and if the 
number of non-minor candidates is greater than the number of victors. 

The definition of a minor candidate is inherently subjective. Previous work in the 
literature has excluded candidates who receive fewer than 5\% of the overall vote, 
while other studies have focused exclusively on contested inbument held seats in 
open jurisdictions \cite{krebs, oliver}. Due to the diversity of school board 
electoral organization, school district size, and the relatively low number of votes 
necessary to secure a victory, I define minor candidates as those candidates who 
receive fewer than 30 votes on the ballot. This is a liberal enough interpretation 
of candidate seriousness to include write-in challenges and has the potential to 
bias results downward. However, with the median winning candidate needing only 
\Sexpr{iNum(median(dat$votes[dat$winner ==1]))} votes to secure victory, 30 votes 
seems sizable enough to exert influence on the race overall. 


<<candidatedescriptives4, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Annual share of contested school board elections under two different measures of candidate contestation.">>=
library(scales)

# 
races$contested <- 0
races$contested[races$nrealcand > races$nwins & 
                          races$nwins > 0 & races$electiontype == 1] <- 1
races$contestedSer <- 0
races$contestedSer[(races$nrealcand - races$nminor) > races$nwins & 
                          races$nwins > 0 & races$electiontype == 1] <- 1
#  incumbents challenged
races$contestedInc <- 0
races$contestedInc[races$nrealcand > races$ninc & 
                          races$ninc > 0 & races$electiontype == 1] <- 1
races$contestedSerInc <- 0
races$contestedSerInc[(races$nrealcand - races$nminor) > races$ninc & 
                          races$ninc > 0 & races$electiontype == 1] <- 1
# 


a <- table(races$contestedSer, races$year)
a.tmp <- prop.table(a, margin = 2) 
a <- t(a)
a.tmp <- t(a.tmp)
a <- as.data.frame(a)
a.tmp <- as.data.frame(a.tmp)
a$Var1 <- as.numeric(as.character(a$Var1))
a.tmp$Var1 <- as.numeric(as.character(a.tmp$Var1))

p1 <- qplot(factor(Var1), Freq, data=a[a$Var1 > 2002 & a$Var1 < 2013,], geom='bar', stat='identity', fill=Var2, position='stack', color = I("black")) + 
  labs(x= "Year", y = "Districts", title = "Excluding Minor Candidates", 
       fill = "Contested") + theme_bw()

p1 <- qplot(factor(Var1), Freq, data=a.tmp[a.tmp$Var1 > 2002 & a.tmp$Var1 < 2013,], 
            geom='bar', stat='identity', fill=Var2, position='stack', color = I("black")) + 
  labs(x= "Year", y = "Percent of Races", title = "Excluding Minor Candidates", 
       fill = "Contested") + theme_bw() + scale_y_continuous(label = percent)


a <- table(races$contested, races$year)
a.tmp <- prop.table(a, margin = 2) 
a <- t(a)
a.tmp <- t(a.tmp)
a <- as.data.frame(a)
a.tmp <- as.data.frame(a.tmp)
a$Var1 <- as.numeric(as.character(a$Var1))
a.tmp$Var1 <- as.numeric(as.character(a.tmp$Var1))


p2 <- qplot(factor(Var1), Freq, data=a[a$Var1 > 2002 & a$Var1 < 2013,], geom='bar', stat='identity', fill=Var2, position='stack', color = I("black")) + 
  labs(x= "Year", y = "Races", title = "Including Minor Candidates", 
       fill = "Contested")+ theme_bw()

p2 <- qplot(factor(Var1), Freq, data=a.tmp[a.tmp$Var1 > 2002 & a.tmp$Var1 < 2013,], 
            geom='bar', stat='identity', fill=Var2, position='stack', color = I("black")) + 
