       fill = "Contested") + theme_bw() + scale_y_continuous(label = percent)


grid.arrange(p1, p2, main = "Comparing School Board Contestation Measures")
@


% Compare other measures of contestation, number of candidates per seat, etc. 

Figure \ref{fig:candidatedescriptives4} depicts the pattern of school board candidate 
emergence over time. Contested elections are in blue and uncontested races are in 
salmon. A slight trend toward fewer contested races is evident in both panels. 
The bottom panel, excluding minor candidates, changes the share of elections 
that are contested each year, but does not change the pattern across years. 



<<perseatcontests,  echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Serious candidates per open seat by school year in Wisconsin school districts over time.">>=

races$candPerSeat <- races$nrealcand / races$nwins

qplot(factor(year), candPerSeat, 
      data=races[races$year > 2001 & races$year < 2013,], geom='boxplot') + 
  theme_bw() + 
  labs(x="Year", y="Candidates per Open Seat", 
       title = "Candidates Per Open Seat on Wisconsin School Boards") + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 2, linetype = I(2), color = I("blue")) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = I(5), color = I("red"), size = I(1.025))


@

Another way we might measure the level of candidate emergence in school board races 
would be to look at the number of candidates per seat. This measure would 
differentiate between districts with wide spread contestation and those with 
simply a single contested seat. As Figure \ref{fig:perseatcontests} demonstrates, 
the level of contestation per open seat does not change much across the years with 
the median race having 1 candidate per seat, and the majority of races having between 
1 and 2 candidates per seat. This measure does not appear to capture much information 
beyond a binary indicator for the presence of more than one candidate per race. 

\subsubsection{Incumbent Defeat}

Traditionally, the school board literature has not been concerned as much with 
overall levels of contestation, but instead the frequency with which incumbents 
are defeated. Incumbent defeat is a good measure of the democratic functioning 
of the school district as a government because it reflects the ability of voters 
to exercise a preference for change and to ``throw the bums out''. %TODO: citation
Incumbent defeat is at the heart of the dissatisfaction theory of school board 
participation \cite{RefWorks:297}. Previous research has found that apolitical 
forms of board member turnover often swamp political forms of turnover and skew 
results \citep{RefWorks:311}. These studies used surveys of former board members 
and asked whether their reason for no longer serving was political or apolitical -- 
allowing the observation of candidates who chose not to run due to political pressure 
or a perception of a strong emerging challenger. This is a limitation of the current 
study is identifying seat 
vacancies due to retirement or plausible threat where a candidate does not mount a 
campaign. This is less troubling in the school board case because the cost of 
campaigning is so low that presumably a challenged incumbent would still stand 
for election.



<<incdefeat, echo=FALSE, fig.cap="Annual share of Wisconsin school board elections featuring an incumbent with a defeated incumbent 2002-2012.">>=

races$nincDefInd <- ifelse(races$nincDef >0, 1, 0)

a <- table(races$nincDefInd[races$ninc > 0], races$year[races$ninc > 0])
a.tmp <- prop.table(a, margin = 2) 
a <- t(a)
a.tmp <- t(a.tmp)
a <- as.data.frame(a)
a.tmp <- as.data.frame(a.tmp)
a$Var1 <- as.numeric(as.character(a$Var1))
a.tmp$Var1 <- as.numeric(as.character(a.tmp$Var1))

qplot(factor(Var1), Freq, data=a.tmp[a.tmp$Var1 > 2002 & a.tmp$Var1 < 2013,], 
            geom='bar', stat='identity', fill=Var2, position='stack', color = I("black")) + 
  labs(x= "Year", y = "Percent of Races", title = "Percentage of Races with Incumbents Defeated", 
       fill = "Inc. Defeat") + theme_bw() + scale_y_continuous(label = percent)


@

Figure \ref{fig:incdefeat} shows the rate of incumbent defeat in Wisconsin. The 
greatest share of races featuring an incumbent that had incumbent defeat is 
\Sexpr{iPer(max(a.tmp$Freq[a.tmp$Freq < 0.5])*100)}. As we saw in 
Chapter \ref{chap:aboutwisco} this rate of incumbent defeat is lower than other 
forms of office, but it is not as low as might be expected. \citet{oliver}'s study 
of the suburban voter found 10 out of 11 mayoral incumbents securing re-election 
and 47 out of 56 city council incumbents winning re-election. This result is encouraging 
and it suggests the potential to explore theories for why voters may prefer 
challengers over incumbents, including retrospective voting. 

In the next section I turn my attention to how to leverage 
this unique data to test competing theories of when contestation for a seat on a 
school board in Wisconsin will emerge. 

\clearpage

\section{Methods}

To assess the probability of a contested election in a school district in a given 
election cycle, we start with a model of the emergence of candidates:

\[ (1) \hspace{0.25 in}  \Pr_{CE_{t}} = \alpha + \beta X + \epsilon \] \\

Here, $\Pr_{CE_{t}}$ represents the probability that a school board seat is contested 
in an election in year \emph{t}. The $X$ represent a vector of measures of conditions 
in the school district leading up to the time candidates choose to run for office, 
the period between the previous election and the current election. Estimating 
$\beta$ provides a way to test for the importance of each of the conditions in 
the school districts. 

The first way to estimate $\Pr_{CE_{t}}$ is by estimating a logistic regression. 
Logistic regression allows for the estimation of a binomial outcome using linear 
predictors. In this case, the linear predictors will be composed of the variables 
discussed above in Table \ref{tab:candIV}. I estimate the logistic regression using 
the maximum likelihood estimator available in the generalized linear model,  
\texttt{glm}, functions in R \cite{cran}. 

However, this logistic regression is not correctly specified as it ignores the 
dependence between observations inherent in the Wisconsin data. Each school district 
is observed between two and ten times, meaning that the assumption of independent 
and identically distributed random variables is likely violated. To correct for this, 
I employ sandwich estimators of the variance-covariance matrix to extract robust 
standard errors for these models, which will correct for the interdependence between 
the observations and avoid overly optimistic tests for statistical significance 
among the $\beta$ coefficients. I estimate the robust standard errors using 
the \texttt{lmtest} and \texttt{sandwich} packages for R \cite{sandwich, sandwich2, lmtest}. 

I fit four separate models following this approach, looking at the impact of 
demographic, fiscal, political, and policy characteristics of school districts on 
the probability of a contested school board race. I then fit a full model which 
includes all of the variables from the above models. 

This approach still makes some assumptions about the structure of the data that 
are not ideal. Most importantly, this approach compares each school district year 
to all other school district years in the dataset and ignores any explicit 
interdependence on prior years within the same district. This fails to take advantage 
of the repeated measures available for each school district due to the panel nature 
of the dataset and reduces the importance of large changes in $X$ values within a 
district that are still relatively small when compared across all districts. 

There are a number of approaches available to estimating longitudinal and panel 
data. As the panel here is not balanced, the observations within districts are 
not evenly spaced due to the fact that districts may not have open seats on their 
board each year, I adopt a multi-level modeling approach that assumes district 
elections are nested within districts. This approach, also known as hierarchical 
linear modeling, is flexible for cases with unbalanced repeated measures and 
allows the analyst to disentangle the effects of time specific variables from the 
overall variability among individual units, e.g. school districts \cite{mlmod}. 
One way to depict this approach is in the equation:

\begin{align*} 
\Pr_{CE_{ij}} &= \alpha_{i} + \beta X_{i} + \gamma_{j} + \epsilon   \\ 
\gamma_{j} &=  \alpha_{j} + \zeta Z_{j}
\end{align*}

In the model I use \texttt{i} for the specific elections nested within the school 
districts, \texttt{j}. Here the $\gamma_{j}$ terms represent independent intercepts for each school 
district as predicted by $Z_{j}$, a vector of school district characteristics 
that are time invariant. $X_{t}$ is a vector of time variant school district 
characteristics, some of which may be lagged. In the mixed-effect 
framework the $\gamma_{j}$ are assumed to be drawn from a normal distribution that 
is explained by the linear combination of $\alpha$ and $\zeta$. 

This method does not account for the time structure of the data, as no 
explicit parameterization of time is included. As I have no reason to believe that 
time linearly related to the probability of an election being contested, this is 
appropriate. However, I do want to control for secular effects of election year 
variability that reaches across districts. One approach would be to include 
fixed effects for years in the $X$ vector. However, the multilevel approach is 
flexible enough to allow for a third group -- years, to be included in the model. 
The model below includes a third grouping level for years. 

\begin{align*} 
\Pr_{CE_{ij}} &= \alpha_{i} + \beta X_{i} + \gamma_{j} + \lambda_{t} + \epsilon   \\ 
\gamma_{j} &=  \alpha_{j} + \zeta Z_{j}
\lambda_{t} &= \alpha_{t}
\end{align*}

This approach allows year parameters to be estimated independently without assuming 
a functional form for the relationship between the probability of candidate emergence 
and time. This is beneficial as there does not appear to be a single time trend. 
It also results in estimates the $\beta$ coefficients that are conditional 
on the group specific variances captured in the $\gamma$ and $\lambda$ vectors. 
This allows us to take advantage of the repeated measure structure of the data and 
avoid overconfidence in estimates resulting from comparisons within and across groups. 
The multilevel approach is not common in longitudinal applications in political 
science, but has been used by \citet{oliver} to understand the relationship between 
individual survey respondents and their communities. The models are fit following the 
guidance of \citet{gelhill} using the \texttt{lme4} package in R \citep{lme4, cran}. 

The only difference between this approach and the generalized linear models above 
is that there are group-level terms. For the mixed model approach, I also fit the 
four models -- demographic, fiscal, political, and policy. The mixed models will 
increase the accuracy of over the pooled models. Next, I need a way to compare 
the models to evaluate the variables that best explain the emergence of candidates. 
Model comparison is difficult, especially in the case of binomial regression where 
more traditional measures like $R^{2}$ are not available. Instead of a model 
based measure like deviance, I instead focus on correct classification -- the 
ability of models to correctly assign observations as either a contested or 
uncontested election. 

Since my observations are unbalanced, with many more uncontested than contested 
elections, I need a measure of correct classification that is robust to class 
imbalance. For this, I use the receiver operator characteristic, or ROC, curve 
which provides a measure of the tradeoff between detection (true positive identification) 
and false alarm rates across different values of the cutoff of the probability 
estimate \citep{hanley, zwieg}. Of particular value is the 
area-under-the-curve which summarizes the accuracy of the model across all values 
of the probability cutoff. As we are modeling a discrete 
event -- whether or not a challenger emerges in a school district board election -- 
it makes most sense to judge model fit using a measure of how well the model 
accurately classifies elections into types -- contested and uncontested. 



